      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the probability that when four fair 6-sided dice are rolled, they won't all show the same number, we can use the complementary probability approach. This involves calculating the probability that all four dice show the same number and then subtracting this from 1.\n\n1. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when four dice are rolled. Since each die has 6 faces, the total number of outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. Determine the number of favorable outcomes where all four dice show the same number. There are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6), and for each number, there is exactly one outcome where all four dice show that number. Therefore, the number of favorable outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6\n   \\]\n\n3. The probability that all four dice show the same number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{6}{1296} = \\frac{1}{216}\n   \\]\n\n4. The probability that not all four dice show the same number is the complement of the probability that all four dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{216}{216} - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when four fair 6-sided dice are rolled, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
