      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that four fair 6-sided dice won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, and since the dice are rolled independently, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:**\n   There are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (all showing 1, all showing 2, all showing 3, all showing 4, all showing 5, or all showing 6). So, there are 6 outcomes where all dice show the same number.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number:**\n   This is the total number of possible outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1296 - 6 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number:**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{107}{108}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{107}{108}}\n\\]",
